from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "5. The Twelve, the Seventy and Healing in Acts 1 to 8"),
("p", "Claim: the Lord sent the Twelve and the Seventy to heal, and He sent them to Israel. The book of Acts records the Twelve doing it in Jerusalem, in Judaea and in Samaria, before the people of Israel, and the passages that explain the healings explain them in Israel’s terms. The chapter reads the commissions and then the healings in Acts 1 to 8 in order. It reports what each says the healing was for and what each leaves unsaid."),
("sub", "5.1 The Twelve are sent"),
("p", "“And when he had called unto him his twelve disciples, he gave them power against unclean spirits, to cast them out, and to heal all manner of sickness and all manner of disease” (Matthew 10:1). The commission follows at once: “These twelve Jesus sent forth, and commanded them, saying, Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not: But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel. And as ye go, preach, saying, The kingdom of heaven is at hand. Heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, raise the dead, cast out devils: freely ye have received, freely give” (Matthew 10:5–8)."),
("p", "The commands are in a plain order. Go to the lost sheep of the house of Israel. Preach that the kingdom of heaven is at hand. Heal the sick. The healing is part of the work of the Twelve in the places to which they are sent, and the places are Israel’s. Luke gives the same commission in his own words: “Then he called his twelve disciples together, and gave them power and authority over all devils, and to cure diseases. And he sent them to preach the kingdom of God, and to heal the sick” (Luke 9:1, 2). Mark reports what they did: “And they went out, and preached that men should repent. And they cast out many devils, and anointed with oil many that were sick, and healed them” (Mark 6:12, 13). The Twelve anointed the sick with oil in the cities of Israel, and Chapter 8 returns to that fact."),
("sub", "5.2 The Seventy"),
("p", "“After these things the Lord appointed other seventy also, and sent them two and two before his face into every city and place, whither he himself would come” (Luke 10:1). The instruction repeats the order: “And into whatsoever city ye enter, and they receive you, eat such things as are set before you: And heal the sick that are therein, and say unto them, The kingdom of God is come nigh unto you” (Luke 10:8, 9). The Seventy go before the Lord’s face to the places He is about to visit. They heal, and they say that the kingdom of God has come near. When they come back the Lord names what they should rejoice in: “Notwithstanding in this rejoice not, that the spirits are subject unto you; but rather rejoice, because your names are written in heaven” (Luke 10:20)."),
("sub", "5.3 The sentence in Mark 16"),
("p", "The last chapter of Mark has a sentence that names the laying on of hands: “they shall lay hands on the sick, and they shall recover” (Mark 16:18). Study 25 reports the passage and its textual question, and Volume 1 reads the commission in chapter 6, with its audience and the tense of its confirmation. This volume cites those readings and does not argue the passage again. It records only that the verse falls in the commission the Eleven receive and that it is followed by the sentence, “And they went forth, and preached every where, the Lord working with them, and confirming the word with signs following” (Mark 16:20)."),
("sub", "5.4 The healings in Acts 1 to 8"),
("p", "The book of Acts gives the Twelve their ground at the start: “But ye shall receive power, after that the Holy Ghost is come upon you: and ye shall be witnesses unto me both in Jerusalem, and in all Judaea, and in Samaria, and unto the uttermost part of the earth” (Acts 1:8). The first healing the book records is at the temple. Peter and John went up “at the hour of prayer,” and a man “lame from his mother’s womb” was laid daily at the gate called Beautiful (Acts 3:1, 2). Peter said, “In the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth rise up and walk,” and “immediately his feet and ankle bones received strength” (Acts 3:6, 7)."),
("p", "Peter’s explanation is addressed to Israel. “Ye men of Israel, why marvel ye at this? or why look ye so earnestly on us, as though by our own power or holiness we had made this man to walk?” (Acts 3:12). He says, “And his name through faith in his name hath made this man strong, whom ye see and know” (Acts 3:16). Before the council he says again that the man is made whole “by the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth” and addresses “ye rulers of the people, and elders of Israel” and “all the people of Israel” (Acts 4:8–10). The council’s answer is: “that indeed a notable miracle hath been done by them is manifest to all them that dwell in Jerusalem; and we cannot deny it” (Acts 4:16)."),
("p", "The believers then pray about the healing in the same terms. “And now, Lord, behold their threatenings: and grant unto thy servants, that with all boldness they may speak thy word, By stretching forth thine hand to heal; and that signs and wonders may be done by the name of thy holy child Jesus” (Acts 4:29, 30). The prayer asks for boldness to speak, and for the Lord’s hand to heal and for signs and wonders, as two requests together. The next chapter records the result: “And by the hands of the apostles were many signs and wonders wrought among the people; (and they were all with one accord in Solomon’s porch” (Acts 5:12). The sick were brought out “into the streets,” “that at the least the shadow of Peter passing by might overshadow some of them,” and “a multitude out of the cities round about unto Jerusalem” brought sick folk, “and they were healed every one” (Acts 5:15, 16)."),
("p", "The healings pass beyond the Twelve. “And Stephen, full of faith and power, did great wonders and miracles among the people” (Acts 6:8). In Samaria, Philip preached Christ, and “the people with one accord gave heed unto those things which Philip spake, hearing and seeing the miracles which he did,” and “many taken with palsies, and that were lame, were healed” (Acts 8:6, 7). Samaria is within the commission of Acts 1:8, and the apostles at Jerusalem sent Peter and John to it (Acts 8:14)."),
("sub", "5.5 What the healings in Acts 1 to 8 say they are for"),
("p", "The passages say several things about the purpose. The healing at the temple is done in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth and is explained to the men of Israel as done by faith in that name (Acts 3:16). It becomes the occasion of Peter’s address and of the council’s examination (Acts 4:9, 10). The prayer of Acts 4:30 asks that signs and wonders be done “by the name of thy holy child Jesus.” The Spirit’s power is promised for witness, and the healings sit beside the speaking of the word. Peter’s own explanation, “as though by our own power or holiness,” names the healing as the Lord’s work and not the apostle’s."),
("p", "The passages also leave things unsaid. They do not say that any believer who is sick may seek healing as a gift. They do not say that the healings are to be repeated in every assembly of believers. The healings are done “by the hands of the apostles” (Acts 5:12), by Stephen, and by Philip, among the people of Israel and of Samaria, in the period of the book that Volume 1 reads as the Prophecy line. The volume reports the healings as the book reports them. It does not suggest that the Twelve’s healings carry any promise to those who are not in the commission of Acts 1:8."),
("tbl", "Healing in Acts 1 to 8.", [
    ("Acts 3:6, 7", T("Acts 3:6, 7"), "The first healing recorded: a lame man at the temple, in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth."),
    ("Acts 3:12", T("Acts 3:12"), "Peter addresses the men of Israel and names the source."),
    ("Acts 4:30", T("Acts 4:30"), "The believers pray for the Lord’s hand to heal and for signs and wonders."),
    ("Acts 5:16", T("Acts 5:16"), "A multitude from the cities round about is healed."),
    ("Acts 8:7", T("Acts 8:7"), "In Samaria many with palsies and the lame are healed."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The Twelve are told to go to the lost sheep of the house of Israel, preach the kingdom and heal the sick.",
    "The Seventy are sent before the Lord’s face to heal and to say that the kingdom of God is come nigh.",
    "The healings in Acts 1 to 8 are done by the Twelve, by Stephen and by Philip, in Jerusalem, Judaea and Samaria.",
    "Peter explains them to Israel as done by the name of Jesus Christ and not by the apostles’ own power.",
    "The passages record no instruction to believers in general to heal or to seek healing.",
  ]),
]
